Fixed in `tokio/src/sync/batch_semaphore.rs:376`.

**Cause:** the permit counter packs the `CLOSED` flag into bit 0 (`PERMIT_SHIFT = 1`). `forget_permits` read the count, shifted it back up, and stored `new << PERMIT_SHIFT` — a value with bit 0 always zero. So any successful `forget_permits` on a closed semaphore cleared the closed flag, and `try_acquire`/`is_closed`/`acquire` behaved as if the semaphore were open again (waiters woken by `close()` were already gone, and `Waitlist::closed` stayed `true`, so the two halves of the state also disagreed).

**Change:** carry the flag bit through the CAS.

```rust
let new = curr.saturating_sub(n);
// Preserve the `CLOSED` flag stored in the low bit of the counter;
// forgetting permits must not reopen a closed semaphore.
let new_bits = (new << Self::PERMIT_SHIFT) | (curr_bits & Self::CLOSED);
match self.permits.compare_exchange_weak(curr_bits, new_bits, AcqRel, Acquire) {
```

Since the CAS still compares against the full `curr_bits`, a concurrent `close()` between the load and the store fails the exchange and retries with the closed bit visible.

Verification: `forget_closed` fails on the parent commit and passes with the fix; the full `sync_semaphore` suite (18 tests), `sync_semaphore_owned`, `sync_mutex`, `sync_rwlock`, and the in-crate `sync::` unit tests all pass. Note `cargo fmt` isn't installed in this environment, so formatting was matched by hand rather than checked.
